# Image-specific per-package USE decisions.

# Breaks the sys-libs/libcap-ng <-> sys-process/audit build-time cycle.
# The cycle exists only because this profile sets 'bpf' globally: libcap-ng[bpf]
# build-depends on audit, and audit build-depends on libcap-ng. This is
# portage's own suggested cut, and it costs nothing here — libcap-ng's bpf
# support is for its capability-set parser, not for anything audit needs.
sys-libs/libcap-ng -bpf

# Breaks the media-libs/freetype <-> gnome-base/librsvg cycle.
# Portage offers '-svg' or '-utils'; '-utils' is the right cut. freetype's
# dependency is literally 'utils? ( svg? ( librsvg ) )' — librsvg is needed
# only to build the demo programs (ftview, ftbench, ftdump), not for the
# library's OT-SVG support. '-svg' would have cost colour-font rendering for
# nothing. Both flags are on globally: 'svg' from the graphics group, 'utils'
# from 'extras tools utils contrib extra'.
media-libs/freetype -utils

# Breaks the whole family of X -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> X build cycles in one
# cut, rather than one ffmpeg flag per cycle. Every one of them routes through
# the same edge: openal -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> openal, then
# libsdl2 -> pipewire -> ffmpeg -> libsdl2, and so on for anything ffmpeg
# build-depends on that also offers a pipewire backend.
#
# It is also the cheapest of the three cuts portage offers each time. From the
# pipewire ebuild itself:
#     # - ffmpeg is only used for pw-cat. We don't build the spa plugin ...
#     -Dffmpeg=disabled # Disabled by upstream and no major developments to
#                       # spa/plugins/ffmpeg/ since May 2020
# So the flag buys compressed-format support in the pw-cat CLI tool and
# nothing else; the SPA plugin it sounds like it enables is off upstream
# regardless. The alternatives each cost a real audio path: '-sdl' on ffmpeg
# drops ffplay, '-pipewire' on libsdl2 or openal drops their native backend.
media-video/pipewire -ffmpeg

# Overrides host.use, which carries 'x11-libs/libxcb doc' from this host's own
# package.use. That single entry re-enables doc against the profile's global
# '-doc' and builds a cycle out of nothing:
#   doxygen -> graphviz -> libX11 -> libxcb -> doxygen
# libxcb[doc] exists to generate its own API HTML with doxygen. A live image
# has no use for it, and dropping it also takes doxygen and graphviz out of
# the graph entirely.
# (This file sorts after host.use, so it wins; portage reads package.use/* in
# alphabetical order and the last matching entry applies.)
x11-libs/libxcb -doc

# Breaks tslib -> libsdl2 -> pipewire -> libv4l -> qtbase -> tslib.
# x11-libs/tslib is a resistive-touchscreen input library from the embedded
# world; a T14's touchscreen goes through libinput/evdev and never touches it.
# The flag reached qtbase through rare-flags.use, which is where the census
# moved it — this file is named 'zz-' so image decisions sort last and win
# over both host.use and rare-flags.use.
dev-qt/qtbase -tslib

# Breaks libjpeg-turbo -> virtual/jdk -> openjdk-bin -> harfbuzz -> glib
# -> docutils -> pillow -> libjpeg-turbo.
# The global 'java' flag reaching a low-level image codec: libjpeg-turbo[java]
# builds the TurboJPEG Java wrapper, which drags a whole JDK into the graph.
# Nothing in this image loads JPEGs through Java.
media-libs/libjpeg-turbo -java

# Breaks pillow -> harfbuzz -> glib -> docutils -> pillow, the one cycle in
# this image that portage can suggest no cut for: dev-libs/glib BDEPENDs
# dev-python/docutils unconditionally, and docutils BDEPENDs pillow the same
# way, so neither edge has a flag to turn off. Only pillow[truetype] does.
#
# Chosen over a two-pass build (merge pillow once without truetype, let the
# main run rebuild it with it) to keep the pipeline single-pass.
#
# What it costs: PIL/Pillow's ImageFont TrueType rendering, and with it
# app-text/calibre, which USE-depends on pillow[truetype] and is therefore
# dropped from PROF_LIVE_PKGS. matplotlib is unaffected — it carries its own
# freetype. Re-enable the flag and rebuild in the running system if needed.
dev-python/pillow -truetype

# sysbench only supports LuaJIT: its REQUIRED_USE is literally
# 'exactly-one-of ( lua_single_target_luajit )'. The image make.conf sets
# LUA_SINGLE_TARGET="lua5-4" globally (host copy), which makes the ebuild
# unresolvable — a hard 'pkgs' abort, not a warning. luajit is not use-masked
# on the hardened profile, so a per-package target is the whole fix.
app-benchmarks/sysbench LUA_SINGLE_TARGET: -* luajit

# Breaks zziplib -> libsdl2 -> libpulse -> gtk+:3 -> cups -> xdg-utils
# -> xmlto -> texlive-formatsextra -> texlive-core -> zziplib.
# zziplib[sdl] adds an SDL_rwops shim for reading zip archives through SDL.
# Nothing in this image reads zips that way, and the flag is only set because
# 'sdl' is global.
dev-libs/zziplib -sdl

# Breaks sci-ml/gloo <-> sci-ml/caffe2 — the one cycle here that no USE flag
# cuts cleanly: gloo[cuda] BDEPENDs sci-ml/caffe2[cuda], and caffe2[cuda,gloo]
# RDEPENDs '>=sci-ml/gloo-2025.06.04[cuda?,rocm?]', so with cuda on each side
# wants the other built first. This host never hit it only by history — gloo
# has been installed since 2023, long before caffe2, so portage was never
# asked to schedule both at once. A fresh image has no such luck.
#
# Neither alternative survives contact:
#   - 'sci-ml/gloo -cuda' is not a cut. caffe2[cuda] propagates cuda into the
#     dep, and emerge answers 'there are no ebuilds built with USE flags to
#     satisfy ">=sci-ml/gloo-2025.06.04[cuda?,rocm?]"'. Relaxing that dep in
#     ::pwr would not help either: with USE_SYSTEM_GLOO and USE_CUDA both on,
#     pytorch greps the system gloo's config.h for 'GLOO_USE_CUDA 1' and
#     hard-errors at configure time (cmake/Dependencies.cmake:1295).
#   - 'nccl' in place of 'gloo' does not exist for CUDA. caffe2's REQUIRED_USE
#     is 'nccl? ( rocm )' and the flag maps to dev-libs/rccl; no NVIDIA NCCL
#     package is in ::gentoo or ::pwr, which is why this host runs -nccl.
#
# And '-gloo' cannot be the permanent answer either, which is what a probe of
# the overlay set settled on 2026-08-07: dev-python/vllm (::pwr, reached from
# sci-ml/unsloth) RDEPENDs 'sci-ml/caffe2[distributed,gloo]' outright. Its
# ebuild says why — vllm builds a CPU coordination group on the gloo backend,
# and because this caffe2 is USE_NCCL=OFF the device group falls back to gloo
# as well, so without the flag vllm dies at engine init with "Fallback Gloo
# backend is not available" (upstream note: verified 2026-06-14, bug #274).
# sci-ml/accelerate names caffe2[gloo] too, but only in a 'test?' BDEPEND,
# which is inert here.
#
# So there is no flag to set in this file at all. The cut has to be temporary:
# caffe2 is merged ONCE with USE=-gloo before pass 2 (which is all the cycle
# needs — it gives gloo[cuda] something to build against), and pass 2 then
# builds gloo[cuda] and rebuilds caffe2 with gloo back on, in that order.
# That lives in the profile, as
#
#     PROF_LIVE_CYCLE_BOOTSTRAP=( "sci-ml/caffe2 -gloo" )
#
# and costs one extra caffe2 build. Deliberately NOT a '-gloo' line here: a
# permanent flag would resolve, build for hours and produce an image whose
# whole reason for existing (the unsloth/vLLM stack) fails at run time — the
# expensive kind of silent failure.

# net-vpn/cisco-secure-client — the embedded SAML/SSO browser.
#
# PROF_LIVE_USE turns 'webkit' off globally, and the flag defaults to on in
# IUSE, so without this line the image would build a client with acwebhelper
# quietly missing: the merge succeeds, net-libs/webkit-gtk:4 never gets pulled,
# and the failure only shows up at the office, on a headend that insists on the
# embedded browser rather than handing SAML to acextwebhelper.
#
# 'telemetry' is deliberately NOT set: it defaults off, which leaves out
# libacfeedback.so (Customer Experience Feedback). That is the same choice the
# stock installer makes when a deployment sets DisableCustomerExperienceFeedback
# in ACTransforms.xml, so the client stays a supported configuration.
net-vpn/cisco-secure-client webkit

# sys-devel/gcc[pgo] — profiled bootstrap. toolchain.eclass:2281 swaps
# GCC_MAKE_TARGET from 'bootstrap-lean' to 'profiledbootstrap-lean': gcc is
# built, instrumented, trained by compiling itself, then rebuilt against that
# profile. Typical payoff is a ~5-10% faster cc1/cc1plus, which on this fleet
# is not academic — a full image run is ~1800 packages, so the compiler is the
# hot path of every build we do.
#
# Cost, MEASURED on amdkdehard 2026-08-26 (pgo+lto vs the same versions built
# without them): gcc-16.2.0 25m05s -> 50m55s (2.03x), gcc-15.3.1 21m04s ->
# 44m58s (2.13x). So a clean 2x, the pessimistic end of the original estimate.
# Four of
# the five x86 images carry TWO gcc slots (15 for CUDA, 16 for the desktop —
# see the toolchain-split note in amdkde.conf). It is a ONE-TIME cost per
# binpkg pool, though: with --usepkg now on, the profiled gcc is built once
# and reused. Four pools (binpkgs, -amdkdehard shared with nvidiahard,
# -intelhard, -corehard) means the fleet pays for it once, then not again
# until the next gcc bump.
#
# This file is in the 'common' layer, which only the x86 live profiles use
# (the ARM boards go through PROF_BOARD_OVERLAY_DIR = "hardened rpi400kde ..."
# and never see it). That is deliberate: a profiled bootstrap on a cross or
# native ARM build would be a far worse trade.
#
# [lto] joins it (operator decision 2026-08-26, replacing a note here that
# advised against it). --with-build-config=bootstrap-lto builds gcc ITSELF with
# LTO. It is an independent axis from pgo — a different eclass variable
# entirely, BUILD_CONFIG_TARGETS vs GCC_MAKE_TARGET — so neither flag pulls the
# other in; they simply compose.
#
# Worth keeping two things apart that often get conflated: LTO-bootstrapping
# GCC is an upstream-supported build configuration whose risk is mainly memory
# at link time, whereas '-flto' in the global CFLAGS for the whole world is the
# genuinely hazardous one (ODR violations, symbol versioning, ctor ordering).
# This is the first, not the second.
#
# Watch peak RAM on the first build: linking cc1plus under LTO is the high
# point, and this host has taken a global OOM before (caffe2 with -j32).
sys-devel/gcc pgo lto

# sys-devel/binutils[pgo] — TRIED 2026-08-26, REMOVED. Do not re-add without
# reading this first.
#
# Both slots failed identically, in the profile-use phase:
#   ld/ldver.c:35:1: error: source locations for function 'ldversion' have
#                    changed, the profile data may be out of date
#   make[4]: *** [Makefile:1604: ldver.o] Error 1
# Same on ldbuildid.c and, in 2.46.1, ldelfgen.c. That is
# -Werror=coverage-mismatch: gcc found .gcda whose recorded source locations do
# not match the file being compiled. The files it hits are the ones binutils
# generates or rewrites during the build, so their line numbering moves between
# the profile-generate and profile-use phases.
#
# Why it was not worth fixing: the only cheap workaround is
# -Wno-error=coverage-mismatch, which lets the build proceed while USING the
# mismatched profile — paying for a double build and getting PGO driven by bad
# data. The honest fix means debugging the ebuild's interaction with the
# two-phase build, hours of work for a package that builds in 1m27s. Even a
# 10% win there saves ~9 seconds across an 1800-package run.
#
# Also relevant if someone reconsiders: binutils trains on the dejagnu TEST
# SUITE (that is why pgo BDEPENDs dejagnu + bc), which is synthetic, unlike
# gcc's train stage that compiles gcc itself.
